\description{
\code{sequence_compare()} compares how often short subsequences occur in two
or more groups of sequences. For each pattern length in \code{sub} it counts
every k-gram (a run of k consecutive states, such as \code{"A->B->C"}) in
each group, keeps the patterns that are frequent enough in every group,
and returns a \code{net_sequence_comparison} whose pattern table has one row
per pattern with its count, proportion and standardised residual in each
group and, optionally, a permutation or chi-square test of whether its
frequency differs between the groups. It takes a grouped network, a
single network or wide sequences with a grouping.

A residual says how far a group's count of a pattern lies from the count
expected if pattern and group were independent, in approximately
standard-normal units (Haberman, 1973); a positive value means the
pattern is more frequent in that group than independence predicts. The
p-values are adjusted across every pattern tested. The permutation test
treats the sequence as the
unit, while the chi-square test treats every occurrence as independent,
which overstates the evidence when occurrences within a sequence depend
on each other (see \emph{Details}). Patterns of different lengths overlap
(each 3-gram contains two 2-grams), so their tests are not independent of
each other. \code{\link[=sequence_patterns]{sequence_patterns()}} with \code{group} asks the related question
of whether a pattern is present in more sequences of one group.
}
\details{
The standardised residuals come from the 2 x G table of the pattern's
counts against the counts of all other k-grams of the same length in
each group, with the formula
\code{(o - e) / sqrt(e * (1 - r/N) * (1 - c/N))}. They are always
computed, whatever \code{test} is. A k-gram that spans a missing position is
not counted.

\code{test} chooses the inference:
\describe{
\item{\code{"permutation"}}{Shuffles the group labels across
sequences \code{iter} times and recomputes, for each pattern, the
Euclidean norm of its row of differences between observed and
expected counts in the patterns-by-groups table of the patterns kept
at that length (expected under independence). It asks whether the
pattern's distribution is associated with group membership at the
level of the sequence,
which respects the sequence as the unit of analysis but has little
power when there are few sequences. The p-value is
\code{(b + 1) / (iter + 1)}, where \code{b} counts the permutations with a
statistic at least as large as the observed one, so it cannot fall
below \code{1 / (iter + 1)}. The effect size is the observed statistic
minus the mean of the permuted ones, divided by their standard
deviation (taken as 1 when the permuted statistics do not vary; with
\code{iter = 1} the effect size is \code{NA}, since at least two permutations
are needed). The shuffles use R's random number generator: call
\code{\link[base:Random]{base::set.seed()}} first for reproducible p-values.}
\item{\code{"chisq"}}{Runs \code{\link[stats:chisq.test]{stats::chisq.test()}} on each pattern's
2 x G table (with Yates' continuity correction when there are two
groups). It asks whether the groups' streams of k-grams produce the
pattern at different rates, treating every occurrence as an
independent event: it is fast and has power with few sequences, but
the independence it assumes is optimistic when sequences are
strongly autocorrelated.}
\item{\code{"none"}}{No test; the counts, proportions and residuals
only.}
}

The p-values are adjusted once across all patterns of all lengths, with
\code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}}.
}
\section{Nested data and \code{actor}}{

The permutation test treats every sequence as exchangeable. When several
sequences come from the same actor (sessions of one student, students of
one team), they resemble each other, and shuffling single sequences
understates how much the pattern counts vary by chance: when the groups
are made of different actors, the test then rejects too often. \code{actor}
names the column identifying the actor of each sequence, and the shuffle
respects it, as in \code{\link[=permutation]{permutation()}} (Good, 2005; Anderson & ter Braak,
2003): an actor whose sequences all lie in one group keeps them together
and moves as a whole, its group label shuffled among the other such
actors; an actor with sequences in several groups (a within-actor design,
such as pre and post sessions of each student) has its labels shuffled
among its own sequences only, so it keeps its number of sequences in each
group. Mixed designs combine the two. Use it whenever the groups are made
of different actors; for groups within actors it costs little. The
observed counts, proportions and residuals do not change; only the
p-values and effect sizes do. Sequences with a missing group keep it.
With few actors there are few distinct ways to shuffle them, and a
warning of class \code{nestimate_few_actors} is raised when no p-value could
fall below 0.05.

Several columns, outermost first (\code{actor = c("Group", "Student")}), are
nested levels, the unit at each level being the combination of its column
with the ones before it. The labels are then shuffled at the level where
they are assigned, within the units above it, as in \code{\link[=permutation]{permutation()}}: units
whose sequences all lie in one group exchange their labels with the other
such units of the same parent (all top-level units at the first level),
each moving whole; a unit with sequences in several groups is opened at
the next level, and inside such a unit of the last level single sequences
exchange labels. One column runs exactly the one-level scheme above.
}

\section{Plot styles}{

\code{plot()} draws the standardised residuals of the top \code{top_n} patterns,
prints the plot and returns the \code{ggplot} invisibly (\code{NULL}, with a
message, when there are no patterns). \code{style = "auto"} (the default)
picks the style from the number of groups:
\describe{
\item{\code{"pyramid"}}{Back-to-back bars of each pattern's proportion
in the two groups, each bar shaded by that group's standardised
residual (the colour saturates at plus or minus 3), with the
(adjusted) p-value in the gap between the bars when a test was run.
Needs exactly 2 groups.}
\item{\code{"heatmap"}}{One tile per pattern and group, coloured by
the standardised residual (the colour saturates at plus or minus 3)
and labelled with its value. Works for any number of groups.}
}
The residuals are filled whatever \code{test} was chosen, so both styles work
after \code{test = "none"}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
seqs <- data.frame(
  V1 = sample(LETTERS[1:4], 60, TRUE),
  V2 = sample(LETTERS[1:4], 60, TRUE),
  V3 = sample(LETTERS[1:4], 60, TRUE),
  V4 = sample(LETTERS[1:4], 60, TRUE)
)
grp <- rep(c("X", "Y"), 30)
net <- build_network(seqs, method = "relative")
res <- sequence_compare(net, group = grp, sub = 2:3, test = "chisq")
\donttest{
# Students are nested in teams, and Achiever is a team-level label:
# shuffle whole teams, not single students
net <- build_network(group_regulation_long, method = "relative",
                     actor = "Actor", action = "Action", time = "Time",
                     group = "Achiever")
set.seed(1)
sequence_compare(net, sub = 2, iter = 100, actor = "Group")
}

}
